The following code snippet contains a bug:

```php
    public function toOffsetName(DateTimeInterface $date = null)
    {
        $minutes = floor($this->getOffset($date ?: Carbon::now($this)) / 60);

        $hours = floor($minutes / 60);

        $minutes = str_pad((string) (abs($minutes) % 60), 2, '0', STR_PAD_LEFT);

        return ($hours < 0 ? '-' : '+').str_pad((string) abs($hours), 2, '0', STR_PAD_LEFT).":$minutes";
    }
```

The code fails the following tests:

Test `Tests\CarbonTimeZone\ConversionsTest::testToOffsetName with data set #8`:
            
```php
    public function testToOffsetName($date, $expectedOffset, $timezone)
    {
        Carbon::setTestNow(Carbon::parse($date));
        $offset = (new CarbonTimeZone($timezone))->toOffsetName();

        $this->assertEquals($expectedOffset, $offset);
    }
```

The error message is:

```
Failed asserting that two strings are equal.
```
Test `Tests\CarbonTimeZone\ConversionsTest::testToOffsetNameDateAsParam with data set #8`:
            
```php
    public function testToOffsetNameDateAsParam($date, $expectedOffset, $timezone)
    {
        $offset = (new CarbonTimeZone($timezone))->toOffsetName(Carbon::parse($date));

        $this->assertEquals($expectedOffset, $offset);
    }
```

The error message is:

```
Failed asserting that two strings are equal.
```

Please provide a fixed version of the buggy function, and only that function without additional imports. Make sure the code is between ``` and ```.
```php
    public function toOffsetName(DateTimeInterface $date = null)
    {
        $offset = $this->getOffset($date?: Carbon::now());
        $hours = floor($offset / 3600);
        $minutes = floor(($offset % 3600) / 60);

        $minutes = str_pad((string) abs($minutes), 2, '0', STR_PAD_LEFT);

        return ($offset < 0? '-' : '+').str_pad((string) abs($hours), 2, '0', STR_PAD_LEFT).":$minutes";
    }
```
